@page "/finance/profit-summary"
@using Jewel.JPMS.Commercial
@using Jewel.JPMS.Features.Cvr
@using static Jewel.JPMS.Features.Cvr.ProfitDisplay
@using Jewel.JPMS.Features.Commercial
@using Jewel.JPMS.Features.Procurement
@using Jewel.JPMS.Features.Projects
@using Jewel.JPMS.Features.Xero
@inject AuthService Auth
@inject SessionService Session
@inject ProjectListReadModel Projects
@inject ProjectFinancialSummaryReadModel Summary
@inject ProjectWorkOrdersReadModel WorkOrders
@inject ValuationLinesReadModel Lines
@inject ValuationClaimsReadModel Claims
@inject ClaimLinesReadModel ClaimEntries
@inject IValuationInvoiceStore Invoices
@inject XeroSitePnlReadModel SitePnl
@inject IQueryClient Queries
@inject ICommandSender Commands
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@implements IDisposable

<PageTitle>Profit Summary · JPMS</PageTitle>

@*
    The company Profit Summary (the sidebar's Financials folder, finance roles): gross profit by
    project, three ways (finance meeting 2026-08-03), laid out to the accountant's board format
    (2026-08-04) — a summary strip, the budget → forecast bridge, then the table in three bands:

      The deal            — Budgeted Profit only (Initial Contract Sum − Initial Contract
                            Costs, the FinancialSummaryAssumptions target-cost rule). The
                            revised contract sum moved to the forecast band as Final Sales
                            (finance director, 2026-08-17) so that band reads final sales,
                            final cost, forecast profit;
      Current position    — Certified to Date (issued/paid valuation invoices, retention the
                            client holds as a memo) against allocated Xero purchase spend;
      To finish           — what is left in the job from today (added 2026-08-04): the revised
                            contract sum not yet certified, against the cost still to come
                            (forecasted cost of sales less cost to date). Derived, so the three
                            positions reconcile: Current Profit + Profit to Finish = Forecast
                            Profit, to the penny — a job at 10% today finishing at 5% shows the
                            0% still to come;
      Forecast at completion — Final Sales (the revised contract sum, variations as a memo
                            line) against the Financials tab's forecasted cost of sales
                            (committed cost plus remaining drawdown), giving Forecast Profit.

    Every figure reads from the same sources as the per-project tabs, so the two can never
    disagree. The bridge reconciles by construction: Budgeted Profit + Variations + Cost
    movement = Forecast Profit, because Cost movement is defined as Initial Contract Costs −
    Forecasted Cost of Sales. The project filter defaults to live jobs and offers every project.

    Around the table sit the three panels from the accountant's combined mock (2026-08-04/05,
    replacing the reconstructed margin-% lines, which sawtoothed because certification
    arrives in lumps), ALL driven by the same stored Xero site P&L months (the profit & loss
    report filtered by each project's Sites tracking option, stored monthly by
    SyncXeroSitePnl — nightly worker + the cumulative panel's Refresh button), cumulative
    profit to date on Xero figures only (the accountant's call, 2026-08-05 — no forecast
    snapshots). The running grid LEADS the page — above the summary strip, bridge and table
    (Jeremy, 2026-08-13 pm: "an easy one stop shop for what Nigel wants to see as a trend
    for the projects", so the trend comes first); the other two sit under the table:

      Running profit by month — the project-to-date running % at each month end (cumulative
                            operating profit over cumulative turnover, the accountant's
                            "Running % Profit" row), with the month's movement in percentage
                            points as small print beneath (Jeremy's running-balance format,
                            2026-08-13 — "the trend of the whole project rather than each
                            month, as spikes and falls will get lost"; the month's own %,
                            2026-08-12's format, lives on in the hover), and the running %
                            to date as "Position now";
      Trajectory by job   — the running total of the months' profit £, one mini-chart per
                            job on its own scale against its budgeted-profit band;
      Cumulative position — the two lines the running total is the gap between: cumulative
                            invoiced vs cumulative cost, job start to date.

    Profit here is the accountant's operating profit for the site: income less cost of sales
    less overheads tracked to the site (2026-08-12 — so Woodhouse reads −20.7% here exactly
    as it does on his Xero P&L, not the −17.9% gross-only figure).

    All three report the INVOICED rung of the ladder, so they deliberately sit near, not on,
    the table's certified figures. Jeremy asked whether the two should agree (2026-08-13) —
    they shouldn't, for two structural reasons the footnotes now spell out: the Xero panels
    deduct overheads tracked to the site (Woodhouse's −17.9% certified vs −20.7% invoiced is
    exactly its £7,167 site salaries), and they count what is invoiced, which runs ahead of
    certification on deposit-led jobs (Ravenswood: ~£104k invoiced vs £60k certified) and
    behind it where retention is certified but not yet billed. Both panel groups carry a
    basis tag ("certified basis" / "invoiced basis") so the page answers this itself.
*@

<ApprovedSessionGate>
    <section class="px-6 md:px-8 py-8 md:py-12">
        <header class="mb-6">
            <p class="eyebrow mb-1">JPMS · Company</p>
            <h1 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-semibold text-content leading-tight">Profit Summary</h1>
            <p class="text-sm text-content-muted mt-1">
                Gross profit by project — the deal as signed, where each job stands today, and where it lands.
            </p>
        </header>

        @if (projectsFailed)
        {
            <p class="text-sm text-negative">Couldn't load the project list. The details are in the red bar at the top — reload to try again.</p>
        }
        else
        {
            <div class="mb-4 flex items-center justify-between gap-3 flex-wrap">
                @* The filter is a control, so it is never gated: it renders disabled with a
                   loading label until the project list lands (the loading-states convention). *@
                @if (Projects.Current is null)
                {
                    <select disabled
                            class="rounded-lg border border-line-strong bg-surface-field px-3 py-2 text-sm text-content-subtle">
                        <option>Loading projects…</option>
                    </select>
                }
                else
                {
                    <ProjectMultiSelect Projects="@(Projects.Current!)"
                                        Selected="@selectedIds"
                                        SelectedChanged="OnSelectionChangedAsync" />
                }
                @* Disabled while any selected row failed to load: the on-screen banner discloses
                   the zeros, but a workbook travels without it — retry first, then export. *@
                <ExportToExcelButton FileName="Profit summary"
                                     Disabled="@(!TableReady || SelectedProjects.Count == 0 || FailedSelectedProjects.Count > 0)"
                                     BuildWorkbook="BuildExportWorkbook" />
            </div>

            @* ── Running profit by month — FIRST on the page (Jeremy, 2026-08-13 pm: "an easy
               one stop shop for what Nigel wants to see as a trend for the projects, and it
               is live", so the trend leads and the deal/certified table follows). Jeremy's
               running-balance format (2026-08-13): each cell's main figure is the
               project-to-date running % at that month end (the accountant's "Running %
               Profit"), the small print beneath is the month's movement in percentage
               points — the trend of the whole job, so one month's spike or fall can't
               masquerade as the position. The month's own % (the 2026-08-12 format) lives
               in the hover. Profit includes overheads tracked to the site, so the figures
               reconcile with his P&L exactly — Xero data only, no forecast snapshots (the
               accountant's call, 2026-08-05). Rows follow the table's order (GridProjects)
               — Jeremy mis-paired rows when the two disagreed (2026-08-13). A conditional
               region: it appears once the Xero store has answered; the cumulative panel at
               the bottom carries the group's loading gate and error states. ── *@
            @if (selectionInitialised && SelectedProjects.Count > 0
                 && SitePnl.Current is { IsConfigured: true } && MovementFor(GridProjects) is { } movement)
            {
                <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5 mb-6">
                    <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-x-6 gap-y-1 flex-wrap">
                        <div>
                            <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-content-subtle font-semibold">
                                Running profit by month
                                <span class="normal-case tracking-normal font-normal text-content-faint ml-2">— the whole job's % profit to date at each month end, that month's movement beneath · Xero site P&L, @BasisLabel</span>
                            </p>
                            <p class="text-xs text-content-muted mt-1 mb-3">
                                The main figure is the trend of the whole project; the small print is what the month did to it, in points and in £ — spikes and falls live there, so they can't get lost. The last column is where the job stands to date.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        @* The basis switch for every Xero panel on the page (grid, trajectory, cumulative
                           charts) — a control, so never gated. Default OFF: pure Xero, exactly what the
                           accountant reconciles. ON overlays approved timesheet cost that no Xero-approved
                           bill settles yet, so the current month reads the portal's live labour position. *@
                        <label class="flex w-fit items-center gap-2 text-xs text-content-subtle cursor-pointer select-none whitespace-nowrap"
                               title="Adds approved timesheet cost that no Xero-approved bill settles yet — the portal's live labour, marked • per cell. It drops out automatically as bills are approved in Xero, so closed months always converge back to Xero's own figures.">
                            <input type="checkbox" class="cursor-pointer align-middle" @bind="includeLabourAccrual" />
                            Include approved labour not yet billed
                        </label>
                    </div>
                    <RunningProfitTable Movement="movement"
                                        AccrualHover="AccrualHover"
                                        AccrualOwnFor="AccrualOwnFor"
                                        AccrualToDateFor="AccrualToDateFor" />
                    <p class="text-[11px] text-content-subtle mt-3 max-w-3xl">
                        Each cell's main figure is the whole job to that month end — cumulative profit (income less
                        cost of sales less overheads tracked to the site, in Xero) over cumulative invoicing, exactly
                        the accountant's "Running % Profit" row — so the row reads as the project's trend, and the
                        last month always agrees with "Position now" (the £ beneath it is the profit it is made of).
                        The small print is what the month did to that figure, in percentage points, followed by the
                        month's own profit or loss in £; the month's invoicing and own % (his "Current Month %
                        Profit") stay in the cell's hover. "6-mo Δ" is the running %
                        now against six months ago — "—" where the job wasn't invoiced back then. "n/a" is a job with
                        costs but no invoicing at all yet — no honest percentage exists; hover for the £. "—" is a
                        month before anything happened. Young jobs swing wildly by nature — deposits against little
                        cost read absurdly high — so read them by the £ in the hover, not the %. Cell shade follows
                        the month's movement, improving green and worsening red; every cell keeps its numbers, so
                        colour never carries the meaning alone. This grid reports the
                        <span class="text-content-muted">invoiced</span> rung of the ladder and deducts site-tracked
                        overheads, so its figures deliberately sit near, not on, the
                        <span class="text-content-muted">certified</span> figures in the table below — a job with
                        salaries tracked to its site reads lower here, and one with deposits invoiced ahead of
                        certification reads higher.
                        @if (includeLabourAccrual)
                        {
                            <text>The basis switch is ON: months marked <span class="text-amber-400">•</span> include
                            approved labour not yet billed — approved timesheet cost from the portal that no
                            Xero-approved bill settles yet. It is an accrual, not a Xero figure: as each labourer's
                            bill is approved in Xero the cost enters the synced P&L and leaves the accrual in the
                            same moment, so nothing double-counts and closed months converge back to exactly what
                            Xero says. Switch it off before reconciling against Xero itself. </text>
                        }
                        @if (movement.Rows.Any(row => row.Stale))
                        {
                            <text>⚠ A job with no movement for six months usually means stalled or unbilled, not that nothing is happening on site. </text>
                        }
                        @if (movement.Excluded.Count > 0)
                        {
                            <text>@string.Join(", ", movement.Excluded.Select(project => project.Name)) @(movement.Excluded.Count == 1 ? "has" : "have") no Xero site data yet — see the cards below.</text>
                        }
                    </p>
                </div>
            }

            @* ── Summary strip. Stat-tile pattern (the loading-states convention): each tile keeps
               its label and pulses until every selected project has landed, because every figure
               here is a total over the whole selection. The strip is a conditional region: once
               the selection is seeded it only exists while something is selected, so an empty
               selection never shows tiles pulsing for figures that are not coming. ── *@
            @if (!selectionInitialised || SelectedProjects.Count > 0)
            {
                var stats = TableReady && SelectedProjects.Count > 0 ? LoadedRows() : null;
                var statTotal = stats is not null ? TotalsOf(stats) : null;
                var swing = stats?.OrderByDescending(entry => Math.Abs(entry.Row.ForecastSwing)).First();

                <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-3 mb-4">
                    <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface px-5 py-4"
                         title="The deal as signed: every selected project's initial contract sum less its target cost.">
                        <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-content-subtle font-semibold mb-1">Budgeted profit</p>
                        @if (statTotal is null)
                        {
                            <p class="text-2xl font-semibold text-content flex items-center h-8" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-busy="true">
                                <JewelIcon Class="w-4 h-4 animate-jewel-pulse" />
                                <span class="sr-only">Loading budgeted profit</span>
                            </p>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <p class="text-2xl font-semibold @ProfitClass(statTotal.BudgetedProfit)">@Money(statTotal.BudgetedProfit)</p>
                            <p class="text-xs text-content-subtle mt-1">
                                @(statTotal.BudgetedMargin is decimal bm ? $"{Pct(bm)} on " : "")@MoneyCompact(statTotal.InitialContractSum) as signed
                            </p>
                        }
                    </div>
                    <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface px-5 py-4"
                         title="Where the selected jobs land: revised contract sums against forecasted cost of sales.">
                        <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-content-subtle font-semibold mb-1">Forecast profit</p>
                        @if (statTotal is null)
                        {
                            <p class="text-2xl font-semibold text-content flex items-center h-8" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-busy="true">
                                <JewelIcon Class="w-4 h-4 animate-jewel-pulse" />
                                <span class="sr-only">Loading forecast profit</span>
                            </p>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <p class="text-2xl font-semibold @ProfitClass(statTotal.ForecastedProfit)">@Money(statTotal.ForecastedProfit)</p>
                            <p class="text-xs text-content-subtle mt-1">
                                @(statTotal.ForecastedMargin is decimal fm ? $"{Pct(fm)} on " : "")@MoneyCompact(statTotal.ContractValue) incl. variations
                            </p>
                        }
                    </div>
                    <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface px-5 py-4"
                         title="The project whose forecast profit sits furthest from its budgeted profit — the number to ask about first.">
                        <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-content-subtle font-semibold mb-1">Biggest swing vs the deal</p>
                        @if (swing is null)
                        {
                            <p class="text-2xl font-semibold text-content flex items-center h-8" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-busy="true">
                                <JewelIcon Class="w-4 h-4 animate-jewel-pulse" />
                                <span class="sr-only">Loading biggest swing</span>
                            </p>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <p class="text-2xl font-semibold @ProfitClass(swing.Value.Row.ForecastSwing)">@swing.Value.Project.Name</p>
                            <p class="text-xs mt-1 @(swing.Value.Row.ForecastSwing < 0m ? "text-negative" : "text-positive")">
                                @(swing.Value.Row.ForecastSwing < 0m ? "▼" : "▲") @MoneyCompact(Math.Abs(swing.Value.Row.ForecastSwing)) forecast vs budgeted profit
                            </p>
                        }
                    </div>
                </div>
            }

            @* One gate for the bridge and the table together: every bar, row and total is the same
               arithmetic over the same sources, so the region waits for every selected project
               before showing any of it. *@
            <LoadGate IsLoading="@(!TableReady)" Prominent="true" Label="Loading profit figures">
            @if (SelectedProjects.Count == 0)
            {
                <div class="panel p-8 text-center">
                    <p class="text-sm text-content">No projects selected — pick some in the filter above.</p>
                </div>
            }
            else
            {
                var rows = LoadedRows();
                var total = TotalsOf(rows);
                var bridge = BridgeFor(rows, total);

                @if (FailedSelectedProjects.Count > 0)
                {
                    <p class="rounded-lg bg-negative/10 border border-negative/30 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-negative mb-4">
                        The profit figures could not be loaded for @string.Join(", ", FailedSelectedProjects.Select(p => p.Reference)) — those rows show zeros.
                        <button type="button" class="underline underline-offset-2 ml-1" @onclick="RetryFailedAsync">Retry</button>
                    </p>
                }

                @* ── Budget → forecast bridge. Reconciles by construction: the two floating bars
                   are Variations and (Initial Contract Costs − Forecasted Cost of Sales), which
                   sum exactly to Forecast − Budgeted. Skipped while every figure is zero — a
                   chart of nothing is a conditional region, not a gate. ── *@
                @if (bridge is not null)
                {
                    <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5 mb-6">
                        <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-content-subtle font-semibold">
                            Budget → forecast bridge
                            <span class="normal-case tracking-normal font-normal text-content-faint ml-2">— @bridge.Note</span>
                        </p>
                        <div class="relative h-[210px] mt-3" aria-hidden="true">
                            <div class="absolute inset-x-0 border-t border-line" style="top:@(Pc(bridge.ZeroY))%"></div>
                            <div class="absolute inset-0 grid grid-cols-4">
                                @foreach (var bar in bridge.Bars)
                                {
                                    <div class="relative">
                                        @if (bar.ConnectorY is double connector)
                                        {
                                            <div class="absolute border-t border-dashed border-line-strong" style="top:@(Pc(connector))%;left:-25%;width:50%"></div>
                                        }
                                        <div class="absolute left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 w-16 rounded @bar.BarClass" style="top:@(Pc(bar.Top))%;height:@(Pc(bar.Height))%"></div>
                                        <div class="absolute inset-x-0 text-center text-xs font-semibold @bar.LabelClass" style="top:calc(@(Pc(bar.Top))% - 22px)">@bar.Amount</div>
                                    </div>
                                }
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="grid grid-cols-4 mt-2">
                            @foreach (var bar in bridge.Bars)
                            {
                                <div class="text-center">
                                    <p class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-content-muted font-medium">@bar.Label</p>
                                    <p class="text-[10px] text-content-subtle">@bar.Sub</p>
                                </div>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>
                }

                <ProfitTable Rows="rows" Total="total"
                             SortColumn="@sortColumn" SortDescending="sortDescending" OnSort="SortBy" />
                <ProfitTableNotes />
            }
            </LoadGate>

            @* ── Trajectory by job — the running total in £ of the same Xero months the
               grid at the top of the page shows in %, one mini-chart per job against its
               budgeted-profit band. Same model, same row order (GridProjects). ── *@
            @if (selectionInitialised && SelectedProjects.Count > 0
                 && SitePnl.Current is { IsConfigured: true } && MovementFor(GridProjects) is { } trend)
            {
                <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5 mt-6">
                    <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-content-subtle font-semibold">
                        Trajectory by job
                        <span class="normal-case tracking-normal font-normal text-content-faint ml-2">— cumulative profit £, each job on its own scale · Xero site P&L, @BasisLabel</span>
                    </p>
                    <p class="text-xs text-content-muted mt-1 mb-3">
                        One mini-chart per job — shape is comparable, scale is per-job, so one big job can't squash the others flat.
                    </p>
                    <div class="grid gap-3.5 sm:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-3">
                        @foreach (var card in TrajectoriesFor(trend))
                        {
                            <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface-raised px-4 pt-3 pb-2.5">
                                <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2">
                                    <span class="text-xs font-medium text-content">@card.Project.Name</span>
                                    <span class="text-[11px] font-semibold @ProfitClass(card.SixMonthDelta)">
                                        @(Math.Abs(card.SixMonthDelta) < RunningMovement.ZeroThreshold ? "— flat" : $"{DeltaK(card.SixMonthDelta)}k / 6 mo")
                                    </span>
                                </div>
                                <div class="relative h-16 mt-2">
                                    <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full overflow-visible" viewBox="0 0 100 100" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true">
                                        @if (card.BudgetY is double budgetY)
                                        {
                                            <line x1="0" x2="100" y1="@Pc(budgetY)" y2="@Pc(budgetY)"
                                                  stroke="#2a3242" stroke-width="4" opacity="0.55" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" />
                                        }
                                        <polyline points="@card.PathPoints" fill="none" stroke="@card.LineColor" stroke-width="2"
                                                  vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round" />
                                    </svg>
                                    <div class="absolute w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full -translate-y-1/2 translate-x-1/2"
                                         style="background:@card.LineColor;top:@(Pc(card.EndY))%;right:0"></div>
                                </div>
                                <p class="text-[15px] font-semibold mt-1.5 @ProfitClass(card.PositionNow)">@MoneyCompact(card.PositionNow)</p>
                                <p class="text-[10px] text-content-faint mt-0.5">
                                    @(card.Budget is decimal budget ? $"budget {MoneyCompact(budget)}" : "budget not loaded")
                                    @if (card.Stale)
                                    {
                                        <span class="text-amber-400"> · ⚠ no movement 6 mo</span>
                                    }
                                </p>
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-[11px] text-content-subtle mt-3 max-w-3xl">
                        Grey band = budgeted profit for the job — where the deal says it should land. A line short of its
                        band late in a job is the gap still to close; a line above it is margin in hand. Cumulative Xero
                        site profit (income less cost of sales less site-tracked overheads), so young jobs sit below
                        their band by nature.
                    </p>
                </div>
            }

            @* ── Cumulative position — invoiced vs cost, job start to date, straight from
               Xero's site P&L (the accountant's combined mock, 2026-08-04). One card per
               selected job on its own scale; the gap between the lines is cumulative gross
               profit. Gated on its own store, not on the table's per-project loads — the two
               regions load in parallel and reveal independently. ── *@
            @if (selectionInitialised && SelectedProjects.Count > 0)
            {
                <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5 mt-6">
                    <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-x-6 gap-y-2 flex-wrap">
                        <div>
                            <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-content-subtle font-semibold">
                                Cumulative position
                                <span class="normal-case tracking-normal font-normal text-content-faint ml-2">— invoiced vs cost, job start to date · Xero site P&L, @BasisLabel</span>
                            </p>
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-2 text-[11px] text-content-muted flex-wrap">
                                <span class="flex items-center gap-1.5"><span class="inline-block w-3.5 h-[3px] rounded-full" style="background:@InvoicedColor"></span>Cumulative invoiced</span>
                                <span class="flex items-center gap-1.5"><span class="inline-block w-3.5 h-[3px] rounded-full" style="background:@CostColor"></span>Cumulative cost (incl. site overheads@(includeLabourAccrual ? " + accrued labour" : ""))</span>
                                <span class="text-content-faint">· hover for month values</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <Toolbar>
                            <ToolbarButton Icon="refresh" Label="Refresh from Xero" Busy="@pnlSyncing" OnClick="RefreshSitePnlAsync" />
                        </Toolbar>
                    </div>

                    @if (pnlSyncError is not null)
                    {
                        <p class="rounded-lg bg-negative/10 border border-negative/30 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-negative mt-4">@pnlSyncError</p>
                    }
                    @if (pnlSyncNotice is not null)
                    {
                        <p class="rounded-lg bg-amber-400/10 border border-amber-400/30 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-amber-400 mt-4">@pnlSyncNotice</p>
                    }

                    <LoadGate IsLoading="@(SitePnl.Current is null && !pnlFailed)" Label="Loading Xero site figures">
                        @if (SitePnl.Current is null)
                        {
                            <p class="text-sm text-negative mt-4">
                                The Xero site figures could not be loaded. The details are in the red bar at the top — reload to try again.
                            </p>
                        }
                        else if (!SitePnl.Current.IsConfigured)
                        {
                            <p class="text-sm text-content-muted mt-4">
                                Xero isn't connected — add the Xero credentials to the API's app settings and this panel lights up.
                            </p>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            var cards = CumulativeCardsFor(GridProjects);

                            @if (SitePnl.Current.LastSyncedAtUtc is null)
                            {
                                <p class="text-sm text-content-muted mt-4">
                                    No site figures have been pulled from Xero yet — press "Refresh from Xero" above to run the
                                    first sync; the nightly worker keeps it current from then on.
                                </p>
                            }

                            <div class="grid gap-3.5 sm:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-3 mt-4">
                                @foreach (var card in cards)
                                {
                                    <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface-raised px-4 pt-3.5 pb-3">
                                        @if (card.Chart is null)
                                        {
                                            <div class="flex items-center justify-center min-h-[170px]">
                                                <p class="text-[11px] text-content-subtle text-center leading-relaxed max-w-[240px]">
                                                    <span class="block text-amber-400 mb-1">@card.Project.Name ⚠</span>
                                                    @card.Unavailable
                                                </p>
                                            </div>
                                        }
                                        else
                                        {
                                            var chart = card.Chart!;
                                            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2">
                                                <span class="text-[12.5px] font-medium text-content">@card.Project.Name</span>
                                                <span class="text-[10px] text-content-faint whitespace-nowrap">@chart.RangeLabel</span>
                                            </div>
                                            <div class="relative h-[110px] mt-2">
                                                <div class="absolute inset-x-0 bottom-0 border-t border-line"></div>
                                                <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full overflow-visible" viewBox="0 0 100 100" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true">
                                                    <polyline points="@chart.CostPoints" fill="none" stroke="@CostColor" stroke-width="2"
                                                              vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round" />
                                                    <polyline points="@chart.InvoicedPoints" fill="none" stroke="@InvoicedColor" stroke-width="2"
                                                              vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round" />
                                                </svg>
                                                <div class="absolute w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full -translate-y-1/2 translate-x-1/2"
                                                     style="background:@InvoicedColor;top:@(Pc(chart.InvoicedEndY))%;right:0"></div>
                                                <div class="absolute w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full -translate-y-1/2 translate-x-1/2"
                                                     style="background:@CostColor;top:@(Pc(chart.CostEndY))%;right:0"></div>
                                                @* The hover layer the legend promises: one full-height column per month, the
                                                   month's running figures as its tooltip. *@
                                                @for (var index = 0; index < chart.Points.Count; index++)
                                                {
                                                    var point = chart.Points[index];
                                                    <div class="absolute inset-y-0"
                                                         style="left:@(Pc(index * 100d / chart.Points.Count))%;width:@(Pc(100d / chart.Points.Count))%"
                                                         title="@($"{point.Month:MMM yy} — invoiced {MoneyCompact(point.Invoiced)} · cost {MoneyCompact(point.Cost)} · profit {MoneyCompact(point.Invoiced - point.Cost)}")"></div>
                                                }
                                            </div>
                                            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mt-2 flex-wrap">
                                                <span class="text-sm font-semibold @ProfitClass(chart.GrossProfit)">
                                                    @MoneyCompact(chart.GrossProfit)
                                                    <span class="text-[10.5px] font-normal text-content-faint ml-1">cum. profit@(chart.Margin is decimal cumMargin ? $" · {Pct(cumMargin)}" : "")</span>
                                                </span>
                                                @if (chart.Warning is not null)
                                                {
                                                    <span class="text-[10px] text-amber-400">⚠ @chart.Warning</span>
                                                }
                                            </div>
                                        }
                                    </div>
                                }
                            </div>

                            <p class="text-[11px] text-content-subtle mt-3 max-w-3xl">
                                Gap between the lines is cumulative profit — parallel is healthy, converging is eroding,
                                crossed is underwater, both flat is stalled or unbilled. Figures are Xero's own site P&L (sales,
                                cost of sales and overheads tracked to each project's site), so this panel reports the
                                <span class="text-content-muted">invoiced</span> rung of the ladder — it sits near, not on, the
                                table's <span class="text-content-muted">certified</span> figures, and retention appears only
                                where it is tracked to the site in Xero.
                                @if (includeLabourAccrual)
                                {
                                    <text>The basis switch (top of the page) is ON, so the cost line also carries
                                    approved labour not yet billed — portal timesheet cost that drops out as each
                                    bill is approved in Xero. </text>
                                }
                                @if (SitePnl.Current.LastSyncedAtUtc is DateTimeOffset syncedAt)
                                {
                                    <text>Synced from Xero @syncedAt.ToLocalTime().ToString("d MMM HH:mm").</text>
                                }
                            </p>
                        }
                    </LoadGate>
                </div>
            }
        }
    </section>
</ApprovedSessionGate>
